## The equation of a line

The line you probably know from school is $y = kx + q$, where $k$ is the slope and $q$ is where the line crosses the $y$-axis. It has one flaw for games: a vertical line has no slope, so it can't be written that way. The **general equation of a line** covers every line:

$$
ax + by + c = 0
$$

For a line through two points $P_{1}(x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $P_{2}(x_{2}, y_{2})$, one choice of coefficients is

$$
a = y_{2} - y_{1}, \quad b = x_{1} - x_{2}, \quad c = x_{2}y_{1} - x_{1}y_{2}
$$

The vector $(a, b)$ is the line's **normal vector**. It points straight out of the line, perpendicular to it.

<CodeGroup>

```ts
type Line = { a: number; b: number; c: number };

function lineThrough(p1: Point, p2: Point): Line {
	return { a: p2.y - p1.y, b: p1.x - p2.x, c: p2.x * p1.y - p1.x * p2.y };
}
```

```js
// A line is an object like { a: 1, b: -2, c: 5 }, for x - 2y + 5 = 0.
function lineThrough(p1, p2) {
	return { a: p2.y - p1.y, b: p1.x - p2.x, c: p2.x * p1.y - p1.x * p2.y };
}
```

```py
@dataclass
class Line:
    a: float
    b: float
    c: float


def line_through(p1: Point, p2: Point) -> Line:
    return Line(p2.y - p1.y, p1.x - p2.x, p2.x * p1.y - p1.x * p2.y)
```

</CodeGroup>

## Distance from a point to a line

Put any point $(x_{0}, y_{0})$ into the left-hand side of the equation. For a point on the line, you get $0$. For a point off the line, you get a number whose sign tells you which side of the line the point is on, and whose size grows with the distance, multiplied by the length of the normal vector, $\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$. Divide that length out and you have the distance:

$$
d = \frac{|ax_{0} + by_{0} + c|}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}
$$

<CodeGroup>

```ts
function distanceToLine(p: Point, line: Line): number {
	const value = line.a * p.x + line.b * p.y + line.c;
	return Math.abs(value) / Math.hypot(line.a, line.b);
}
```

```js
function distanceToLine(p, line) {
	const value = line.a * p.x + line.b * p.y + line.c;
	return Math.abs(value) / Math.hypot(line.a, line.b);
}
```

```py
def distance_to_line(p: Point, line: Line) -> float:
    value = line.a * p.x + line.b * p.y + line.c
    return abs(value) / math.hypot(line.a, line.b)
```

</CodeGroup>

The sign is useful on its own, too: it tells you which side of a wall the player is on.

## Circle vs line

A circle touches a line when its centre is at most $r$ away from it. With the squaring trick, that's $(ax_{0} + by_{0} + c)^2 \le r^2(a^2 + b^2)$: no root, and no division.

<CircleLine />

<CodeGroup>

```ts
function circleLineCollide(c: Circle, line: Line): boolean {
	const value = line.a * c.x + line.b * c.y + line.c;
	return value ** 2 <= c.r ** 2 * (line.a ** 2 + line.b ** 2);
}
```

```js
function circleLineCollide(c, line) {
	const value = line.a * c.x + line.b * c.y + line.c;
	return value ** 2 <= c.r ** 2 * (line.a ** 2 + line.b ** 2);
}
```

```py
def circle_line_collide(c: Circle, line: Line) -> bool:
    value = line.a * c.x + line.b * c.y + line.c
    return value ** 2 <= c.r ** 2 * (line.a ** 2 + line.b ** 2)
```

</CodeGroup>

This line never ends. Walls and platforms do, which is what the next chapter is about.
